"The prognosis is influenced by several documented factors. Favorable findings include a low-grade prostatectomy (Gleason 3+4, ISUP grade group 2), negative surgical margins, and no invasion of the seminal vesicles. Countervailing factors include the preoperative PSA of 5.3 and the absence of lymph node removal, with nodal status being documented as pNx, which widens uncertainty as nodal sampling is missing. The biopsy finding of a tubular and cribriform pattern is noted. The difference between the biopsy and prostatectomy findings is a point of consideration. Postoperative PSA and its trajectory are critical unknowns that would significantly revise this prognosis. The current heuristic horizon suggests a follow-up period of approximately 73 months without an event. Missing postoperative PSA or follow-up data prevents a more precise assessment of future risk. The fixed estimated horizon is 72.27 months. This represents expected follow-up without an event, not a known recurrence date. The sensitivity range is 46.97–97.56 months; its coverage has not been validated."
